To prevent a 0 (zero) amount from being accepted in both manual entry and bulk uploads, we need to update the backend manual batch creation logic and the frontend submission logic.
*(Note: Your **Bulk Upload Backend** (JournalBulkValidationService.java at line 580) actually **already** has a strict zero-check built in: if (d.amount == null || d.amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0) d.formatErrors.add("Amount cannot be Zero or Null");. So bulk upload is already perfectly protected!)*
Here are the exact replacements to completely block zero amounts for Manual Posting.
### 1. Backend: Block Zero in JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
Find the createBatchRequest method (around line 852). We need to throw an error if the amount is zero.
**Find this block:**
```java
            // Strict Cross-Field Validation for INR
            if ("INR".equalsIgnoreCase(row.getCurrency()) && amount.stripTrailingZeros().scale() > 2) {
                throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("INR amounts cannot have more than 2 decimal places.");
            }

            entity.setAmount(amount);

```
**Replace it with this:**
```java
            // Strict Cross-Field Validation for INR
            if ("INR".equalsIgnoreCase(row.getCurrency()) && amount.stripTrailingZeros().scale() > 2) {
                throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("INR amounts cannot have more than 2 decimal places.");
            }

            // PREVENT ZERO AMOUNT
            if (amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0) {
                throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("Transaction amount cannot be zero.");
            }

            entity.setAmount(amount);

```
